# The build context is the repository root, not ./server, so that the build can
# read .git and report which revision it is running — see server/Dockerfile.
# That makes an allowlist the only safe shape: the repository also holds an iOS
# app, a fastlane build directory and a local database, none of which have any
# business being sent to the Docker daemon.
#
# Everything, then back in only what the build needs.
*
!server/
!.git/

# --- and out again, from inside server/ -------------------------------------
# These repeat server/.dockerignore, which no longer applies now that the
# context has moved up a level. Kept in both files rather than deleted from
# there: `docker build server/` by hand is still a thing somebody might do, and
# it must not bake a developer's database into an image either.

# The live database. Copying it would bake a developer's data — including PB
# settings, which hold the Google OAuth client secret — into the image.
server/pb_data/

# Locally built binaries. macOS/arm64 host builds are useless in a linux image
# and would be overwritten anyway, but they are ~34 MB of context each.
server/peard-server
server/server
server/*.test
server/coverage.out

# Secrets. Never in an image layer: layers are readable by anyone who can pull
# the image. The APNs key reaches the container through PEARD_APNS_KEY_CONTENT.
**/*.p8
**/*.p12
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# Container definitions themselves are not build inputs.
server/Dockerfile
.dockerignore
